sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-32> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Combinators, symbolic systems long known to be universal, show highly complex behavior despite simple rules—for example a length-8 initial expression grows exponentially with seemingly random internal structure—and they can emulate rule 110, suggesting their universality is just associated with general complex behavior.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Combinators show highly complex behavior despite simple rules";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 11, “The Notion of Computation”; section “Universality in Turing Machines and Other Systems” (from p. 706), p. 713. Scope: S and K combinator systems"
}
